IA Wingman platform: licence for SAAF project participants

Copyright (c) 2026 the IA Wingman contributors

1. Who may use this software
   This licence applies to every person and organisation taking part in the SAAF
   project ("participants"). Each participant has the rights below, without having
   to ask for separate permission.

2. What participants may do
   Participants may run, study, copy and modify this software, and share it with
   other participants, for the purposes of the SAAF project, including training,
   experiments, demonstrations and internal evaluation within the project.

3. What participants may not do
   Without the written permission of the copyright holders, nobody may:
   a. publish this software, or any copy or modified version of it, outside the
      SAAF project, for example in a public repository or on a public website;
   b. give it to anyone who is not a participant;
   c. use it in a commercial product or service, or sell it.
   A participant who leaves the SAAF project keeps no rights under this licence
   from that moment and should delete their copies.

4. Conditions
   Copies and modified versions must keep this licence and the copyright notice.
   Modified versions must say that they have been modified.

5. Your own keys and data
   The AI features run on the Anthropic API key that each participant provides,
   under that participant's own agreement with Anthropic. Do not put real
   personal data, customer data or confidential audit material into a copy of
   this software unless your organisation has approved it. The demonstration data
   in this repository is fictional.

6. Third-party components and sources
   This software uses open-source packages, which stay under their own licences,
   and includes parts adapted from SAAF hackathon projects, named in the code
   where they are used. This licence does not change the terms of either.

7. No warranty and no liability
   This software is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. It uses
   artificial intelligence, and its output can be wrong, incomplete or out of
   date. Participants must check it before relying on it, in particular before
   using it in a real audit. To the extent the law allows, the copyright holders
   are not liable for any loss arising from its use.
